# nodemailer is pinned ahead of what next-auth asks for, deliberately.
#
# next-auth@5 declares `peerOptional nodemailer@"^7.0.7 || ^8.0.5"`, and every version
# in that range is covered by GHSA-p6gq-j5cr-w38f — a message option that reaches the
# filesystem and the network. nodemailer 10 fixes it, and pinning it cleared four high
# advisories from the whole tree.
#
# The peer is genuinely irrelevant here: next-auth wants nodemailer only for its Email
# provider, which this app does not use. Mail is sent by lib/auth/mail.ts through its own
# transport (lib/auth/smtp.ts), so nothing in next-auth ever loads it.
#
# Without this line `npm ci` fails on the peer conflict. `npm install` is more permissive
# and succeeded locally, which is exactly how this went unnoticed: every Netlify build
# after the nodemailer upgrade failed at "Install dependencies" while local work carried
# on, and the deployed site silently served the last good commit for hours.
legacy-peer-deps=true
